WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
Why you can’t just add the scores.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:08.813
The tempting first version is alpha * vector_score + (1 - alpha) * bm25_score.

3
00:00:09.093 --> 00:00:14.320
With raw scores this is almost always wrong, because the two numbers live on different scales.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:24.627
A BM25 score is a sum of positive terms, one per matching query word, each bounded by roughly (k1 + 1) times that word’s idf.

5
00:00:24.907 --> 00:00:32.139
So it grows with the length of the query and the rarity of its words, and it changes when the corpus changes, because idf does.

6
00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:35.576
Scores of 5 or 25 are both ordinary.

7
00:00:35.856 --> 00:00:45.221
A cosine similarity is bounded between minus 1 and 1, and for many embedding models the scores of plausible candidates sit in a narrow band.

8
00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:49.320
Add a number that varies by 10 to a number that varies by 0.

9
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.395
05, and the first decides the ranking.

10
00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:53.997
Your alpha of 0.

11
00:00:54.277 --> 00:00:56.347
5 is not the weight you think it is.

12
00:00:56.627 --> 00:01:06.931
The standard repair is to normalize per query, most often with min-max scaling: within each retriever’s result list, map the best score to 1 and the worst to 0.

13
00:01:07.211 --> 00:01:11.136
Weaviate’s relativeScoreFusion, its default since version 1.

14
00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:14.296
24, does this and then takes a weighted sum.

15
00:01:14.576 --> 00:01:20.869
Its documentation makes a fair case for score fusion: scores carry information that ranks throw away.

16
00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:29.000
If one keyword result is far ahead of the rest, a score-based method can keep that gap; a rank-based method sees only “first”.

17
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.419
Min-max has its own failure modes, and you should know them before trusting it.

18
00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:43.363
The best result in each list always becomes 1, even when every result is poor, so a retriever that found nothing relevant still contributes full-strength votes.

19
00:01:43.643 --> 00:01:47.077
The worst result always becomes 0, however good it was.

20
00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:50.493
A single outlier squeezes everyone else toward 0.

21
00:01:50.773 --> 00:01:56.768
And with one result, or tied results, the formula divides by zero, so you need a rule for that case.
